course: dictionary
lesson: 11499
title: Words that shake things up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Agitating» comes from the verb «agitate». Read the words around it to find its meaning, as you would with any new word. The clues in the sentence show which of its three meanings fits.
explain: «The loud drilling was agitating the baby, who could not stop crying.» The clues are crying and could not stop, so agitating means making someone worried or upset.
explain: «Keep agitating the bottle so the juice and water mix together.» Shaking is the clue, so here agitating means shaking or stirring a liquid. «The villagers were agitating for a new library» uses the phrase agitating for, which means campaigning loudly for a change.
fact: Agitate means to shake or stir something, or to make someone feel worried or upset.
fact: «Agitating» can mean making someone worried or upset.
fact: «Agitating» can also mean shaking or stirring a liquid.
fact: «Agitating for» means campaigning loudly for a change, as in «agitating for reform».
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The loud alarm was agitating the sleepy puppy.» What does agitating mean here? | Singing a happy tune | *Making someone worried or upset | Shaking a bottle of juice | Gently sending the puppy to sleep
exercise: «Keep agitating the mixture until the sugar has dissolved.» What does agitating mean here? | Leaving it alone to rest | Painting it bright blue | Freezing it solid | *Shaking or stirring a liquid
exercise: «The workers were agitating for better pay.» What does agitating for mean? | Running a race | Wrapping up a parcel | *Campaigning loudly for a change | Quietly forgetting about the pay
test: «Keep agitating the paint so the colours blend.» What does agitating mean here? | Making the paint go cold | Painting a picture slowly | Hiding the tin away | *Shaking or stirring a liquid
test: «The students were agitating for longer playtimes.» What does agitating for mean? | *Campaigning loudly for a change | Sleeping at break | Eating a snack | Skipping lessons quietly
exam: «The rumours were agitating everyone in the village, who felt anxious all day.» What does agitating mean here? | Cooking a meal | Calming people down | *Making people worried or upset | Writing a story
